

# ==============================================================================
#  <type>(<scope>): <subject>
#
#  Write the subject line above this block, then a BLANK LINE, then the body.
#  Everything starting with # is stripped automatically.
# ==============================================================================
#
# SUBJECT LINE
#   - Imperative mood: "add", "fix", "remove" — not "added" or "adds".
#     Test: "If applied, this commit will <subject>".
#   - Lower case, no full stop at the end.
#   - The whole line, including type and scope, must fit in 72 characters.
#   - Describe the outcome, not the activity.
#
# TYPES
#   feat      A capability that did not exist before          -> MINOR
#   fix       Correcting behaviour that was wrong             -> PATCH
#   docs      Documentation only
#   style     Formatting only, no behaviour change at all
#   refactor  Restructuring with IDENTICAL observable behaviour
#   perf      Measurably faster or lighter                    -> PATCH
#   test      Adding or correcting tests
#   build     Build system, Maven, dependencies, packaging
#   ci        Continuous integration configuration
#   chore     Housekeeping, no source or test change
#   revert    Undoing a commit; say which one and why
#
# SCOPES (optional — omit if the change is project-wide)
#   catalog  inventory  cart     order    payment  account
#   auth     api        persistence       config   deps
#   docs     release
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# BODY  (wrap at 72 columns — the ruler below is exactly 72 wide)
# 123456789012345678901234567890123456789012345678901234567890123456789012
#
#   The body is the point. Explain WHY, not WHAT — the diff already shows
#   what changed, and can never show why.
#
#   A good body answers, roughly in this order:
#     1. What was the situation before? What was broken or missing?
#     2. Why does it need to change? What does leaving it alone cost?
#     3. What approach was taken, and why that one over the alternatives?
#     4. What consequences would surprise a future reader?
#
#   A one-line commit is acceptable only for genuinely trivial changes,
#   such as fixing a typo. If you cannot think of anything to write, ask
#   whether the subject really is self-evident. Usually it is not.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# BREAKING CHANGES
#   Signal them TWICE:
#     1. A "!" before the colon:   feat(api)!: <subject>
#     2. A footer explaining impact and the migration path:
#          BREAKING CHANGE: <what breaks and what consumers must do>
#
# FOOTERS
#   Closes #12                     closes the issue when merged
#   Fixes #12                      same, conventionally used for bugs
#   Refs #12                       references without closing
#   Co-authored-by: Name <email>
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# BEFORE COMMITTING
#   [ ] ./mvnw clean verify passes
#   [ ] Tests cover the new or changed behaviour
#   [ ] CHANGELOG.md [Unreleased] updated if behaviour changed
#   [ ] Only files related to this one issue are staged
#   [ ] No secrets, credentials, tokens or personal data
#
# Full specification: docs/COMMIT_CONVENTION.md
# ==============================================================================